---

In 1993 *The New Yorker* ran a cartoon of a dog at a computer telling another
dog, ["On the Internet, nobody knows you're a dog."](https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/1993/07/05)

# Summary

- An AI agent is a program that acts for you. It reads your email, books a
  trip, writes code, or buys something.
- Many agents use your own logins, so whatever the agent does looks like you
  did it.
- Agents can be fooled. A stranger can hide an instruction in an email or a
  web page, and the agent may follow it with everything you are allowed to
  do. Agents also make plain mistakes.
- A limited key reduces the damage. A key here is a permission, not a
  password. Like a car's valet key, it opens only what it must, and unlike a
  valet key, it can be switched off.
- Keys cannot tell a person from a bot. That takes a sign-in, a payment, or
  someone who vouches for you, checked once when you first ask for access.

# Agents with borrowed power

An agent gets your power when you type in your password, approve its request
to connect to an account, or install it on your computer. Installed agents
often run with all your permissions, held back only by approval pop-ups.

- **February 2026.** Moltbook, a social network where AI agents post to each
  other, left its database readable by anyone. Researchers
  [found about 1.5 million agent logins](https://wiz.io/blog/exposed-moltbook-database-reveals-millions-of-api-keys)
  and private messages holding keys for other services.
- **February 2026.** One link could take over OpenClaw, a popular personal
  agent. Opening it
  [leaked the agent's login, switched off its "ask me first" setting, and ran commands](https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-25253)
  on the owner's computer. Version 2026.1.29 fixed it.
- **October 2026.** One email to an inbox connected to the Manus agent
  [became code running inside the agent](https://salt.security/blog/how-we-hijacked-an-ai-agent-with-a-single-email),
  next to its logins for the owner's Google Drive and GitHub. The owner only
  had to ask it to check the inbox. Manus fixed the flaw.
- **October 2026.** Researchers asked an agent on Amazon's agent cloud, in
  plain words, to
  [hand over its own access keys](https://labs.zenity.io/post/agentcorruption-initial-imds-access),
  and it did. Their permissions also reached other agents in the same region. Amazon
  called this "documented and expected."

The first two were ordinary bugs. In the last two, a stranger's words steered
an agent that already held real power. Narrower keys would have limited the
reach.

# What you can do

- Never type a password into an AI tool. Use the service's own "connect" or
  "allow access" button, and read what it asks for.
- Choose the smallest permission offered. "See your calendar" is very
  different from "read, send, and delete your email." If the only choice is
  everything, use a separate account, or a card with a low spending limit.
- Never let one agent read your private data, read strangers' messages, and
  send things out. An agent that can do all three can be talked into leaking
  your data or spending your money. A helper that answers customer email
  should not also see your invoices or bank.
- Install few plugins and add-ons. Each one often runs with the agent's full
  power.
- Check connected apps a few times a year. In your Google, Apple, or
  Microsoft account settings, search for "connected apps" and remove what you
  do not recognize.
- If something goes wrong, remove the app's access, change the password, and
  call the number on the back of your bank card if money moved.

If you build agents or websites:

- Give each agent its own narrow key for each task, one that expires and can
  be switched off. Deciding how narrow is the hard part.
- Keep keys out of prompts and logs, where the model, or anyone reading the
  logs, can copy them.
- Make the agent ask before it spends, sends, or deletes, and keep that
  setting out of its reach.
- Record who holds which key, and check identity where a stranger first asks
  for one.

# The confused deputy

Tymshare rented out computer time. Its compiler, a program that turns code
into software, could write into the company's own folder, where the bills
were kept. One customer told the compiler to write its debugging notes under
the billing file's name. The compiler did, and the bills were gone.

The compiler broke no rule. It worked for two bosses, the customer and the
company, and could not tell whose permission it was using. A 1988 paper,
[*The Confused Deputy*](https://doi.org/10.1145/54289.871709), gave the fix:
the customer hands the compiler a key to the one file it may write.

Prompt injection, hiding instructions for an AI in text it reads, is a similar
problem, and a harder one. An agent that holds your logins and reads
strangers' email also works for two bosses. But the model itself cannot
reliably tell an instruction from text it is only meant to read, and no key fixes that. A narrow key
only limits the damage.

# Valet keys

A valet key starts the car but will not open the trunk. A capability is a
valet key for software: a reference to one thing, such as one folder or one
calendar, where holding it is the permission. Nobody checks your name. A
well-built capability system adds two things a car key lacks:

- The owner can switch one key off without touching the others, like
  re-keying one lock after a key goes missing.
- A holder can make a weaker key for a helper, never a stronger one.

The idea dates to [a 1966 paper](https://doi.org/10.1145/365230.365252). It
works well inside systems built around it, but it never replaced the
everyday permission list, which answers "who can get in here?" at a glance.
Agents make a stronger case for keys, because a program that can be talked
into anything should hold as little as possible.

glowcap is a code library for programmers.
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) is a network protocol that carries
capabilities between computers, and glowcap implements it.
[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) walks through an example.
[*The Heart of Spritely*](https://files.spritely.institute/papers/spritely-core.html#revocation-accountability)
builds a key that keeps a log and can be switched off.

# What limited keys fix

Give every person and agent its own key, and a service can answer three of
these four questions.

| Question | With limited keys |
| --- | --- |
| Who is responsible for this request? | Whoever was given the key it came with. |
| How do I stop it? | Switch off that one key. Nobody else is locked out. |
| How much harm can it do? | Only what that key opens. |
| Is this one person, or ten thousand bots? | Keys cannot tell. |

Keys have limits of their own.

- A stolen key works for the thief. Guard keys like cash. Short lives, narrow
  reach, and an off switch limit the loss.
- A key names who is responsible, not who acted. If Bob lends his key to
  Mallory, the record still says Bob.
  [*Horton's "Who Done It?"*](https://www.usenix.org/legacy/event/hotsec07/tech/full_papers/miller/miller.pdf)
  (2007) shows one way to follow responsibility through many hands.
- A record of who is responsible is also a record of what they did. Someone
  holds that record, and it can be read, leaked, or demanded.
- Switching a key off stops only what comes next. It cannot undo what the key
  already did, or take back what was read or copied.

# Agents that act for you

"Allow this app to see your calendar" is the familiar first step: the app
gets a key to your calendar, not your password. ("Sign in with Google" only
tells the app who you are.) Capabilities carry the same idea through every
handoff. You give your agent a weaker key, and it gives a helper a weaker key
still.

[Alice gives her agent a key that reads and posts, and the agent gives a helper a key that only reads. The helper reads the chat room, and the request traces back to Alice's key. Alice switches off the agent's key. The helper's next read is refused, because its key came from the agent's, and Alice still posts as herself.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-identity-delegation.svg'))

- Every request traces back to whoever granted the key, as long as each
  helper gets a new key instead of borrowing one. That shows who handed out
  the power, not who sent the request.
- Switching off one key can stop every key made from it, when each handoff
  goes through a link the owner can cut. A holder who shares the same key, or
  relays requests, sits outside that chain.

# Counting people

A bot operator can make ten thousand accounts in a minute. Proving that each
account is a different real person is called Sybil resistance, after a 1973
book about a woman said to have many personalities. A
[2002 paper](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-45748-8_24) showed that in an open
network, one party can pretend to be many unless someone trusted vouches for
who is real.

Cheap filters slow bots without naming anyone. One makes each message cost a
little computer work, an
[idea from 1992](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48071-4_10) that Bitcoin later
borrowed. Another is the [CAPTCHA](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-39200-9_18)
puzzle, which programs often solve better than people.
Rate limits stop careless bots, but determined ones spread across thousands
of internet addresses. Filters cut down bot traffic, but none of them can say
how many real people are left.

## A number for every person

India's [Aadhaar](https://uidai.gov.in/) numbers more than a billion
residents, and Estonians sign contracts and file taxes with a national ID
card. Both work at national scale, and a registered identity makes it far
easier to find who answers for what happened. Both also show the costs.

1. A number can be copied or leaked, so it needs a secret only its owner
   holds. In 2017 a flaw in how the card chips made their secret keys led
   Estonia to block and renew the certificates on about 760,000 ID cards.
2. The registry decides who can prove who they are. When a fingerprint scan
   fails, the person is turned away. A
   [large study](https://doi.org/10.1162/rest_a_01296) of food rations in the
   Indian state of Jharkhand found that fingerprint checks alone did not cut
   theft, and 1.5 to 2 million legitimate people lost benefits for a time,
   largely because of how the change was rolled out. In 2018 India's Supreme Court upheld Aadhaar
   for welfare and taxes but barred private companies from requiring it.
3. One registered person can still run ten thousand bots. An ID says who
   answers for the traffic, not how much there is.
4. One number everywhere lets organizations join their records. People with
   good reason to stay hidden, such as someone escaping an abuser, lose the
   most.

## Proving one fact

A middle path is to prove one fact when you first ask for access. A zero-knowledge proof
shows that a statement is true and reveals nothing else, the way a go/no-go
gauge confirms a part is within tolerance without showing the drawing.
Combined with blind signatures, which let a checker sign something without
seeing it, it lets you show "a trusted checker says I am over 18" without
showing who you are. Both ideas come from the 1980s
([1982](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-0602-4_18),
[1985](https://doi.org/10.1145/22145.22178)).

The checker still decides who counts, and people it cannot check are left
out. Those hardest to give a credential to, such as people without papers,
are often the ones who most need to stay unnamed.

The European Union's
[digital identity wallet](https://ec.europa.eu/digital-building-blocks/sites/display/EUDIGITALIDENTITYWALLET/EU+Digital+Identity+Wallet+Home),
which [EU law](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1183/oj) requires every
member state to offer by the end of 2026, is built toward this model for
facts like age and residence. As of October 2026, how well it hides you from
checkers who compare notes depends on technical choices still open. Because
each service can see a different name, the wallet alone cannot show that a
visitor signed up only once.

# The front door

Put the identity check in one place: the moment a stranger first asks for a
key.

[Alice and a swarm of ten thousand bots both head for a service. A quick check runs before the service does any real work, and bots without a pass are turned away there. Alice gets through, shows the front door one proof, and leaves with her own key, which can post ten times a minute and can be switched off.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-identity-front-door.svg'))

1. A quick check comes first: a cap on requests per minute, or an anonymous
   pass showing the visitor proved itself elsewhere.
   [Privacy Pass](https://doi.org/10.1515/popets-2018-0026) (2018) was built
   to spare people using the Tor privacy network from endless CAPTCHAs, and its
   architecture became [RFC 9576](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9576.html)
   in 2024.
2. Strangers can reach only the front door.
3. The door asks for one proof: a payment, a sign-in, a credential, or an
   introduction from someone already trusted.
4. The door hands back a key with its own limits and its own off switch.

A proof slows fakes only if it costs something real, and accounts sold in
bulk weaken even that. A real cost also turns away honest people: those
without a bank account, without a document the door accepts, or unable to
solve its puzzle. Whoever runs the door decides which proofs it accepts.

Past the door, the service checks each request against the key it carries.
You still rely on whoever issues, stores, and switches off those keys.

# Who pays when an agent gets it wrong

A record of what an agent did does not decide who pays for it. Terms of service often put the loss
on the user. Card rules treat a charge you did not authorize differently from
one you did, and whether a fooled agent's purchase counts as authorized is
unsettled. Laws on agents differ by country. Good records help settle a
dispute, but they help whoever holds them, and users rarely hold the
service's logs.

# Standards for agents

As of October 2026, most of these are drafts or company programs.

- **Bots that say which bot they are.** A
  [September 2026 draft](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-webbotauth-httpsig-protocol/)
  from the IETF, the group that writes internet standards.
- **Bots that prove someone checked them, without saying who they are.** A
  [July 2026 draft](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-rescorla-anonymous-webbotauth/)
  that also caps each one's requests.
- **Agents acting for you.** OAuth, the standard behind "allow access"
  buttons, can record who acted for whom
  ([2020](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8693.html)).
- **Agents that shop.**
  [Visa](https://usa.visa.com/about-visa/newsroom/press-releases.releaseId.21716.html)
  and
  [Mastercard](https://newsroom.mastercard.com/news/press/2025/april/mastercard-unveils-agent-pay-pioneering-agentic-payments-technology-to-power-commerce-in-the-age-of-ai)
  (2025) let shopping agents prove what they are and pay with stand-ins for
  your card number.
- **Agents that pay small amounts.** The web set aside the code
  `402 Payment Required` [in 1997](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2068.html)
  "for future use." The
  [x402 Foundation](https://www.linuxfoundation.org/press/linux-foundation-announces-operational-launch-of-x402-foundation-to-standardize-internet-native-payments-for-ai-agents-and-applications),
  launched in July 2026, puts it to work.

The upside of these efforts is that they fit the web as it is. Naming bots also
lets a site admit only the bots it recognizes, which favors large operators
and can shut out small tools, including assistive agents some disabled people
rely on.

A capability system builds narrow passes, spending caps, and records of who
acted for whom into the key itself, as
[Macaroons](https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2014/programme/macaroons-cookies-contextual-caveats-decentralized-authorization-cloud/)
(2014) did for web passes. It helps only where both sides speak it.

# glowcap and OCapN

glowcap covers the keys on this page and none of the identity checks.

- An encrypted connection checks that the computer that answered holds the
  key you dialed.
- A server caps how many connections can be opening at once.
  [Known limitations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/boundaries.md)
  lists where these protections stop.
- [Loam]($link.page('loam')), glowcap's storage layer, offers managed access.
  It hands out keys that can be switched off and never grow stronger when
  passed on, and records who holds each one.

OCapN leaves the front door, the identity check, and tracing responsibility
to each application.

# A short history

| Year | Work | What it added |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1966 | [*Programming Semantics for Multiprogrammed Computations*](https://doi.org/10.1145/365230.365252) | The capability: a protected reference that is the permission to use what it names. |
| 1975 | [*The Protection of Information in Computer Systems*](https://doi.org/10.1109/PROC.1975.9939) | Least privilege, and permission lists compared with keys. |
| 1982 | [*Blind Signatures for Untraceable Payments*](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-0602-4_18) | Signing something without seeing it. |
| 1985 | [*Security without identification*](https://doi.org/10.1145/4372.4373) | Credentials that cannot be traced back to you. |
| 1985 | [*The Knowledge Complexity of Interactive Proof-Systems*](https://doi.org/10.1145/22145.22178) | Zero-knowledge proofs. |
| 1988 | [*The Confused Deputy*](https://doi.org/10.1145/54289.871709) | Why a program should act with the permission it was handed, not its own. |
| 1992 | [*Pricing via Processing or Combatting Junk Mail*](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48071-4_10) | Making bulk messages cost the sender computing work. |
| 1997 | [Hashcash](http://www.hashcash.org/papers/hashcash.pdf) | Computing-work stamps made practical, later borrowed by Bitcoin. |
| 1999 | [*SPKI Certificate Theory*](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2693.html) | Permissions granted to a key and passed on in signed certificates, with no name in between. |
| 2002 | [The Sybil paper](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-45748-8_24) | Without someone vouching, one party can pose as many. |
| 2003 | [*CAPTCHA: Using Hard AI Problems for Security*](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-39200-9_18) | Puzzles to tell people from programs. |
| 2007 | [Horton](https://www.usenix.org/legacy/event/hotsec07/tech/full_papers/miller/miller.pdf) | Following responsibility as a key passes from hand to hand. |
| 2014 | [Macaroons](https://www.ndss-symposium.org/ndss2014/programme/macaroons-cookies-contextual-caveats-decentralized-authorization-cloud/) | A web pass any holder can narrow and none can widen. |
| 2018 | [*Privacy Pass*](https://doi.org/10.1515/popets-2018-0026) | Anonymous passes that replace repeated CAPTCHAs. |
